Ladybird gives each Fetch request a serial that every hop of a
redirect shares, the way Firefox’s WebRequest.sys.mjs keys requests
by the channel’s id across redirects (ChannelWrapper) and Chromium’s
WebRequestEventRouter keeps one request id for a request’s whole life.
The resource types and their mapping follow Firefox’s list, including
imageset for an image-set candidate and xmlhttprequest for both fetch()
and XMLHttpRequest; Firefox’s object_subrequest is gone from its
ResourceType, so Ladybird doesn’t have it either.
